Introduction 
Pacific Salmon populations exhibit extraordinary life history diversity, shaped by complex and heterogeneous environments across their range (Brannon et al. 2004). The enormous diversity of salmon life history traits (Waples et al. 2004) evolved in response to spatiotemporal variation in both biotic and abiotic conditions (Stearns 1976) and acts to reduce risks of demographic failure by buffering populations against environmental change and exploitation (Hilborn et al. 2003, Greene et al. 2009; Schindler et al. 2010; Beamish 2016). Chinook salmon (Oncorhynchus tshawytscha) display an especially diverse demographic portfolio characterized by a broad array of phenotypes, including variation in age and timing of seaward migration, duration of freshwater and marine residence, ocean distribution, age and season of spawning migration, and timing of spawning (Healey 1991). 
Among these traits, spawning phenology has important ecological and evolutionary consequences. The timing of adult migration and spawning can shape reproductive isolation among temporally segregated groups and lead to fine-scale local adaptation (Manhard et al. 2017). While run timing (entry into freshwater) is often used to define populations segments (Brannon et al. 2004; Waples et al. 2004), here we focus on spawn timing (the date adults deposit fertilized eggs in gravel), as a fine-scale expression of phenotypic variation. Streams provide spatially heterogeneous natal habitats essential for salmon spawning and rearing (Jacobs et al. 2023). Time of spawning is determined by season of entry into freshwater, length and speed of river migration, and time spent in a pre-spawning stage (Ricker 1959). Salmon spawn timing is a key trait in population divergence because it reflects adaptation, strongly influences other traits, and augments spatial isolation of populations (Quinn et al. 2001). Spawn timing varies within and across salmon populations and has a well-documented genetic component (Ricker 1972; Quinn et al. 2002; Beulke et al. 2023). For example, high site fidelity and limited straying spatially segregates related individuals in proximate habitats leading to fine-scale genetic structuring (Neville et al. 2006). Spatial heterogeneity in habitat conditions likely promotes this diversity by selecting for locally adapted phenologies (Ruff et al. 2011). 
Temperature strongly influences salmonid spawn timing (Hendenon 1963; Morrison and Smith 1986; Beechie et al. 2008). Because embryonic development rates are mechanistically driven by ambient temperature (Sparks et al. 2025), salmon tend to spawn at times that ultimately synchronize fry emergence with conditions that optimize offspring growth and development (Quinn 2018; though see Sparks et al. 2019). Several studies have linked site-specific thermal regimes to observed variation in spawning phenology (Sheridan 1962; Burger et al. 1985; Heggberget 1988; Lisi et al. 2013). However, although the importance of temperature as a determinant of spawn timing is well established, the underlying landscape features that may drive thermal variation (i.e., physiography, geomorphology, and hydrology), are poorly understood (Montgomery 1999; O’Sullivan et al. 2019). Geomorphic attributes including slope, elevation, confinement, and groundwater connectivity can shape reach-scale thermal regimes, thereby constraining or enabling different spawning strategies (Beechie et al. 2006, 2008; Jonsson and Jonsson 2011). Lisi et al. (2013), for example, demonstrated that variation in watershed slope, elevation, and lake area explained a large proportion of summer stream temperature and timing of Sockeye salmon (Oncorhynchus nerka) spawning in Alaskan streams, highlighting geomorphology’s indirect but powerful role in structuring phenological diversity.  
The Middle Fork Salmon River (Middle Fork) supports one of the most intensively monitored wild Chinook salmon populations in the Columbia River Basin. Long-term redd surveys by state, federal, and tribal agencies provide a uniquely rich dataset for examining environmental drivers of spawning phenology. The Middle Fork supports wild, indigenous Chinook salmon populations unaltered by direct hatchery supplementation (Thurow et al. 2020) and the watershed is managed as a designated wild anadromous fish sanctuary (IDFG 2019). Minimal anthropogenic effects and a geographic size that facilitates natural processes (i.e., wildfire, floods, debris flows, and snow avalanches) ensure generation and maintenance of diverse environmental conditions (Isaak et al. 2003; Thurow 2015). These natural processes function relatively unimpeded by humans because most of the watershed lies within designated Wilderness. As a result, natal salmon habitats remain diverse, high quality, and connected, and exotic fishes are relatively uncommon (Thurow 2000; Isaak and Thurow 2006; Thurow 2015). 
	Understanding the nature and causes of phenological diversity is of critical importance to recovery of Pacific salmon (Taylor 1991; Waples et al. 2004). We leveraged the Middle Fork’s unique characteristics and robust dataset to examine how variation in stream temperature and habitat attributes influence Chinook salmon spawn timing across multiple years in several tributaries. Characterizing variation in natural processes within stream networks and understanding how spatial and temporal variability in physical processes and Pacific salmon interact are critical, both for establishing recovery targets (Bisson et al. 2009), and for evaluating population persistence under changing environmental conditions (Isaak et al. 2018). Our objectives were to: 1) describe stream reach-scale physical attributes and Chinook salmon spawning phenology from 2002 to 2005, 2) summarize variation in spawn timing across four years in eight streams, and 3) fit a series of linear mixed-effects models to identify associations between environmental covariates and spawn timing. Our goal is explicitly descriptive — to characterize the structure and environmental determinants of spawn timing variation within this system at fine spatial resolution — rather than to develop a transferable predictive framework. The reach-level detail afforded by this approach provides a foundation for future comparative work across broader geographic scales. 

Methods
Study Area
The Middle Fork drains ~7,330 km2 of Central Idaho, predominantly within the Frank Church River of No Return Wilderness, and is one of eight original National Wild and Scenic Rivers designated in 1968 (Figure 1; NWSRS 2016). From its headwaters the Middle Fork flows 171 km before joining the Salmon River near Salmon, Idaho (USFS 1998; Minshall et al. 1981; Thurow et al. 2020). Approximately 800 km of mainstem and tributary habitat are accessible to Chinook salmon (Mallet 1974; Thurow 1985). Detailed study area information can be found in Minshall et al. (1981); Thurow (2000); Servheen et al. (2001); and Thurow et al. (2020).
Adult Chinook salmon migrate from the Pacific Ocean up the Columbia and Snake rivers to spawn in the Middle Fork, typically arriving in early summer and spawning from late July to mid-September. Salmon in this system express a stream-type life history, with most juveniles rearing for one year in freshwater before outmigration (Healey 1991). However, fish exhibit multiple freshwater (age 0-2) and saltwater (1-5) ages, in addition to non-migrating precocial males (Gebhards 1960; James et al. 1998; Copeland and Venditti 2009). Observations suggest that early-arriving adults tend to spawn at higher elevations and later arrivals spawn progressively lower in the basin (Fulton 1968; Gebhards 1959; IDFG 1992; Parkhurst 1950). 
Despite the Middle Fork’s high quality natal habitat, its Chinook salmon are at high risk of extirpation (NOAA 2017). These fish belong to the Snake River spring/summer Chinook salmon Evolutionarily Significant Unit (ESU), listed as “Threatened” under the Endangered Species Act (ESA; NMFS 1992). Current salmon populations average 1-3% of the abundances that annually returned into the mid-1960s (Thurow et al. 2020). Altered migration corridors are considered a proximate cause of salmon declines in the upper Snake River basin in recent decades (Jacobs et al. 2022). NOAA (2022) stated that the centerpiece recovery action is restoring more normalized reach-scale hydrology and hydraulics, and thus river
conditions and function in the lower Snake River and emphasized the urgency of actions is accentuated by climate change. 

Reach selection, Chinook salmon redd counts, and spawn timing
From 2001-2005, we selected study reaches in six major drainages (Big, Bear Valley, Camas, Loon, Marsh, and Sulphur creeks) and included Beaver Creek (tributary to Marsh Creek) and Elk Creek (tributary to Bear Valley Creek) to total eight salmon spawning tributaries that encompass a range of variation in elevation, stream size, and spawn timing. To support collaboration and improve efficiency, some study reaches overlapped with existing index reaches monitored annually by the Idaho Department of Fish and Game (IDFG), the Nez Perce Tribe, Shoshone Bannock Tribe, or the U.S. Forest Service (see Thurow et al. 2020 for index reach methods and locations). Spawning occurs across a broad elevation range, from <920 m in the lower mainstem to >2000 m in tributary headwaters. After selecting reaches, surveyors designated and marked subsections where redds would be identified and georeferenced.
Redd surveyors intensively monitored Chinook salmon spawning from the onset of redd construction through the completion of spawning. From late July through mid-September, trained observers walked stream banks within each study reach on 3-4-day intervals and searched for newly constructed redds. Surveyors began searching for redds before the onset of spawning and georeferenced redd locations with GPS units. To avoid commission errors during the initial survey, surveyors recorded all prior year redds or redd-like streambed features that might have been confused with newly constructed redds. Based on our prior observations in the Salmon River basin, female Chinook salmon typically remain on excavated redds for 3-5 days. Consequently, we assumed our 3-5-day survey interval maximized the likelihood of detecting all redds soon after their construction. 
	Based on our survey intervals, we inferred spawn timing from the first date a redd was fully constructed. Redd locations were spatially joined to stream reaches in the NHDPlus dataset (Moore et al. 2019) and assigned a common identifier (COMID). This identifier was used to link each redd with environmental covariates measured in or derived from the corresponding stream reach (i.e., COMID). We excluded data from 2001, and from Knapp and Cape Horn creeks (Marsh Creek tributaries) because of inconsistent sampling. The final dataset contained 3,016 georeferenced redds (i.e., inferred spawn date) across 104 stream reaches (i.e., COMIDs), nested within the eight spawning tributaries listed above, and spanning four years. 

Covariates 
To identify environmental predictors of spawn timing, we linked each redd location to stream temperature, discharge, elevation, and slope at the stream reach scale (COMID). Covariates were selected based on ecological relevance, full coverage across the study area, and low collinearity (Appendix A1, Section 1.2). Elevation and slope were obtained from NHDPlus (Moore et al. 2019). We indexed thermal conditions using modeled daily stream temperatures at the COMID level from Siegel et al. (2023) after confirming strong agreement with empirical temperature data measured in Idaho (Figure A1.7). Stream discharge data were drawn from a single USGS gage (13309220) near the basin outlet. For each redd, we calculated sequential time-windowed summaries of temperature and flow—i.e., 30-, 60-, and 90-day averages prior to, during, and following the inferred spawn date. 
	Initially, we established our  modeling framework using exploratory data to evaluate variation in spawn timing across years and streams, examine bivariate relationships between spawn date and continuous covariates, and assess pairwise correlations among predictors (Appendix A2). Based on these results, we retained 90-day antecedent temperature (temp_90) as the sole thermal covariate; shorter windows (30- and 60-day) were highly collinear with temp_90 and showed weaker bivariate relationships with spawn date. Post-spawn and fixed-date summaries were weakly associated with spawn timing excluded from further consideration. Stream slope showed no meaningful relationship with spawn date and was excluded. Although 90-day antecedent streamflow (flow_90) was initially considered, it was derived from a single downstream USGS gauge and therefore lacked spatial resolution across the study reaches; moreover, because year-to-year variation in flow dominated its signal, flow_90 was strongly collinear with the year fixed effect (VIF > 1000 in models including both), making independent inference impossible. We therefore excluded flow_90 to avoid misleading attribution of year-level variation to reach-scale flow conditions. 

Data analysis
We used linear mixed-effects models to evaluate environmental predictors of Chinook salmon spawn timing, with redd observation day-of-year as the response variable. Continuous covariates were scaled (mean = 0, SD = 1) to aid convergence and model interpretation. All models included a random intercept for `COMID` to account for repeated measures across stream reaches.  Based on residual diagnostics from initial models and biological expectations of nonlinear thermal responses—including a potential plateau at warm temperatures—we evaluated multiple functional forms for the temperature effects, including quadratic and natural spline terms. 
We fit 31 additive models representing all combinations of fixed effects (temp_90, stream, year, slope, and mean elevation; Appendix A3.1). Models additionally including elevation (m26, m31) had the lowest AIC among candidate models and predicted later spawning at higher elevations — a direction consistent with the expectation that greater migration distance and flow constraints delay access to upstream sites once thermal variation is accounted for. However, these models were excluded from further consideration because moderate collinearity between elevation and stream identity (VIF ≈ 6.5) introduced instability in the fixed effect estimates, and because the within-stream elevation signal that elevation as a fixed effect was attempting to capture is expressed more flexibly through the reach-level random effects structure of the final model (Appendix A4.5.1). Model selection therefore identified the additive model including quadratic temperature, stream, and year (m16) as the most interpretable and parsimonious base model, with nearly identical predictive accuracy and conditional R² compared to m26 (Appendix A3.1). We then assessed whether temperature effects varied across stream reaches by extending m16 with COMID-specific random slopes for temp_90 — yielding the random structure (1 + temp_90 | COMID) — and compared models fit with restricted maximum likelihood (REML). Given the substantial improvement with random slopes (ΔAIC = 510), we next evaluated whether the quadratic temperature formulation adequately captured the nonlinear temperature–spawn timing relationship. We compared the quadratic random slope model to models using natural spline terms (ns(temp_90)) with 3 and 4 degrees of freedom, fit using maximum likelihood. The df = 3 natural spline substantially outperformed the quadratic (ΔAIC = 139) with improved residual behavior, while the df = 4 spline produced biologically implausible predictions at warm temperature extremes indicative of overfitting and was excluded (Appendix A3.4). We therefore selected the natural spline with df = 3 as the final temperature functional form. Finally, we evaluated whether fixed-effect interactions between temperature and either stream or year improved model performance. The temperature × stream interaction produced severely implausible predictions for data-sparse streams (spline extrapolation outside observed temperature ranges), and the temperature × year interaction, while showing large AIC improvement, appeared to capitalize on year-specific data ranges rather than a stable biological effect (Appendix A3.5). Both interaction terms were therefore excluded. The final model — yday ~ ns(temp_90, df = 3) + stream + year + (1 + temp_90 | COMID) — was refit with REML for parameter estimation, diagnostics, and interpretation. Model selection was based on AIC, and model performance was evaluated using marginal and conditional R², RMSE, and intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC).
To examine whether the basin-wide intercept-slope correlation was also expressed as within-stream elevation gradients, we plotted reach-level random intercepts and slopes against mean reach elevation within each stream and fit ordinary least-squares regressions within each stream. Because Bear Valley, Beaver, Marsh, and Sulphur are high-elevation systems with constrained elevation ranges across sampled reaches, within-stream elevation gradients in those streams are difficult to interpret independently of their uniformly high watershed position; we focus interpretation on streams spanning broader elevation gradients. These regressions were performed on model-derived random effects (BLUPs) and do not propagate mixed-model uncertainty; results are therefore treated as exploratory.

Results 
Spawn timing variation in space and time 
Across all streams and years, Chinook salmon spawning exhibited a broadly unimodal distribution, with most activity concentrated between mid-August and early September (Figure 2A). The global mean spawn date was day 240 (August 28th), although year-specific means varied by nearly three weeks (Figure 2A). Stream-level spawning distributions revealed additional spatial structure (Figure 2B), with some streams (e.g., Bear Valley and Beaver creeks) exhibiting narrower peaks, while others (e.g., Big and Camas creeks) displayed broader or later distributions. Year effects were also evident (Figure 2C), with 2005 skewed later than preceding years.
When examined jointly by stream and year, density curves illustrated that each stream expressed a characteristic spawn timing “signature”; although the strength and consistency of the signature varied (Figures A1.3-1.4). Some streams (e.g., Beaver and Marsh creeks) displayed sharp, highly synchronous peaks, whereas others (e.g., Big, Camas, and Elk creeks) exhibited broader, more heterogeneous distributions. These stream-level differences were nested within year effects, reinforcing the combined influence of space and time on diverse spawning phenology.
The cumulative proportion of redds (Figure 3) provided an intuitive view of the pace and duration of spawning. Streams such as Marsh and Sulphur creeks exhibited rapid increases, suggesting short, concentrated spawning intervals. In contrast, Big and Camas creeks displayed more gradual redd accumulations, indicative of extended spawning seasons. Interannual variability was also evident, with some streams shifting substantially across years. Together, these patterns highlight the pronounced spatial and temporal heterogeneity in Chinook salmon spawn timing across the Middle Fork watershed.

Model comparison
We used AIC-based model selection to identify parsimonious models explaining spatial and temporal variation in spawn timing. Among the 31 additive model tested, stream identity, interannual variation, and nonlinear temperature responses were the strongest predictors of Chinook salmon spawn timing (Table 1; Appendix A3.1). Models additionally including elevation (m26, m31) had the lowest AIC among the candidate models and predicted later spawning at higher elevations in a multivariate context—a direction consistent with the expectation that greater migration distance and flow constraints delay access to upstream sites once thermal variation is accounted for. However, elevation was excluded for two reasons. First, the moderate collinearity between elevation and stream identity (VIF ≈ 6.5) introduced instability in the fixed effects estimate. Second, and more fundamentally, the within-stream elevation signal that elevation as a fixed effect was attempting to capture is expressed more flexibly and with greater precision through the reach-level random effects structure of the final model—as confirmed by post-hoc analysis of random intercepts and slopes against mean reach elevation (Appendix Figure A4.8, Table A4.5). Model m16 had nearly identical predictive accuracy (RMSE = 2.02 days) and conditional R² (0.980) compared to m26, despite a modest reduction in marginal R² (0.729 vs. 0.785). Extending m16 to include COMID-specific random slopes for temperature substantially improved model fit (ΔAIC = 510), reduced prediction error from 2.02 to 1.78 days, and slightly increased conditional R², confirming that temperature effects vary meaningfully among stream reaches (Table 1). 
We next evaluated the functional form of the temperature effect within the random slope structure. Residual diagnostics for the quadratic random slope model revealed a systematic trend in residuals across the temperature gradient — overpredicting spawn timing at cold sites and underpredicting at warm sites — suggesting the quadratic term did not fully capture the nonlinear relationship (Appendix A3.3). A natural spline with df = 3 substantially outperformed the quadratic formulation (ΔAIC = 139), with lower RMSE (1.72 vs. 1.78 days) and markedly flatter residuals across the temperature gradient (Appendix A3.4). A natural spline with df = 4 showed further AIC improvement but produced biologically implausible predictions — spawn timing declining sharply above ~13°C — and was excluded (Appendix A3.4). The natural spline with df = 3 was therefore selected as the final temperature functional form.
Finally, we assessed whether interactions between temperature and either stream or year provided additional explanatory power beyond that captured by random slopes. The temperature × stream interaction produced implausible predictions for data-sparse streams (particularly Beaver Creek, where the spline diverged outside its observed temperature range), and the temperature × year interaction, while yielding a large nominal AIC improvement, likely overfitted year-specific data ranges rather than capturing a stable biological effect — marginal R² and RMSE improved only modestly despite the large AIC gain (Appendix A3.5). Both interactions were excluded.
These results led us to retain the natural spline random slope model without interactions as our final: yday ~ ns(temp_90m, df = 3) + stream + year + (1 + temp_90 | COMID), refit with REML. This model balances explanatory power, biological realism, and parsimony, capturing the population-level nonlinear temperature responses via fixed effects while accommodating local deviations in thermal sensitivity via random slopes.

Final model fit and diagnostics
Model fit for the final model was strong: marginal R² (fixed effects only) was 0.599, while conditional R² (fixed + random effects) was 0.990 (Table 2), with RMSE of 1.71 days. The relatively modest marginal R²  reflects the predominance of reach-level random effects—particularly random slopes—in explaining spawn timing variation; the gap between marginal and conditional R² is expected and interpretable, not a deficiency. The high ICC (0.98) confirms that spawn timing is strongly clustered within stream reaches — redds from the same COMID are far more similar to each other than to redds from different COMIDs, reflecting persistent reach-level habitat signatures that structure timing across years. The natural spline model’s marginal R² is modestly lower than the quadratic formulation (0.599 vs. 0.713), reflecting redistribution of explanatory power toward the random effects rather than a loss of predictive performance — conditional R² and RMSE both improved with the spline.
Model diagnostics indicated strong overall performance (Figure A4.1). The posterior predictive check showed excellent agreement between observed and model-predicted distributions of spawn timing. Collinearity was low, with VIFs for all fixed effects below 2. Residual plots revealed mild heteroscedasticity with slight funneling at lower values, likely reflecting skew in early spawn dates rather than model misspecification. The normality Q-Q plot showed moderate tail departures, indicating the Gaussian error assumption is imperfect but sufficient for inference. Random effect Q-Q plots for both intercepts and slopes tracked the diagonal with modest tail departures. DHARMa simulated residual diagnostics flagged several omnibus tests as significant (KS test p ≈ 0, dispersion p = 0.004, outlier p = 1 × 10⁻⁵); with n = 3,016 observations, however, DHARMa's tests readily detect minor deviations that carry little practical inferential consequence. Importantly, residuals plotted against temp_90 showed substantially flatter quantile smoothers compared to the quadratic model, confirming the natural spline more faithfully captured the nonlinear temperature–spawn timing relationship. Persistent residual structure across streams and years reflects fine-scale habitat heterogeneity — groundwater connectivity, hyporheic exchange, local geomorphology — operating below the resolution of available covariates, and is best interpreted as an ecological signal rather than model inadequacy: it represents the very reach-scale processes that motivated this study.

Population-level effects
We estimated marginal mean spawn timing at each factor level and averaged it over the random effects, to provide an overall estimate of the effect in the population. We observed significant differences in spawn timing between many stream pairs (Figure 4A; Table A4.3), particularly in Loon Creek (later spawning) compared to Sulphur Creek (earlier spawning). For example, fish in Loon Creek spawned significantly later compared to fish in Bear Valley, Camas, or Elk creeks, while Sulphur Creek exhibited significantly earlier spawn timing than all other streams except Elk Creek. These patterns reflect spatial heterogeneity in temperature and elevation across streams, that are not fully captured by fixed effects alone.
There was a clear trend toward later spawning over the four-year period (Figure 4B; Table A4.4). Spawning in 2005 occurred significantly later than in all previous years. Although differences between 2002 and 2003 were not statistically significant, later years (2004 and especially 2005) were associated with progressively later mean spawn timing. This temporal shift likely reflects interannual variability in temperature and flow conditions.
Stream-specific predictions revealed a consistent pattern: spawn timing increased nonlinearly with 90-day mean antecedent stream temperature and leveled off at high temperatures (Figure 5). This plateau is consistent with biological expectations, as spawning may be constrained by environmental or physiological thresholds. Stream-to-stream variation in predicted timing reflects both fixed stream effects and COMID-specific random intercepts and slopes. Year-specific predictions similarly exhibited consistent thermal responses across years, with modest variation in average spawn timing because of year effects. 

Group-level effects (COMID deviations from population effects)
Random intercepts and slopes varied considerably among COMIDs, with substantial spread both within and between streams (Figure 6A, 6B). Random effects represent reach-level deviations in baseline spawn timing after accounting for fixed effects: a positive intercept indicates a reach where salmon tended to spawn later than expected given temperature, stream identity, and year; a negative intercept indicates earlier-than-expected spawning. Random slopes represent deviations in thermal sensitivity: steeper slopes indicate stronger temperature dependence, shallower slopes reflect weaker or buffered responses. Among-reach variance was substantial for both baseline timing (τ₀₀ = 128.20) and thermal sensitivity (τ₁₁ = 33.44), and the pattern of variation differed among streams.
The intercept-slope correlation was strongly negative (ρ₀₁ = −0.83; Figure 6C), indicating that reaches with earlier baseline spawn timing exhibited strongly positive thermal responsiveness. Early-spawning reaches are not only earlier — they are also more responsive to temperature variation, suggesting greater phenotypic plasticity. In contrast, later-spawning reaches exhibit shallower or negative thermal responsiveness, possibly reflecting operation near thermal thresholds or stronger constraint by other cues such as photoperiod or migration timing. 
	Figure 6D translates these random effect patterns into predicted temperature–spawn timing relationships for individual COMIDs, illustrating how the combination of varying intercepts and slopes generates a diverse family of reach-specific trajectories around the population mean. While the overall relationship between pre-spawn temperature and spawn timing is positive and nonlinear, the strength and shape of this response varies substantially among reaches, likely reflecting fine-scale differences in hydrology, geomorphology, and local thermal buffering capacity. 
	Significant positive relationships between mean reach elevation and random intercepts were detected in seven of eight streams (Appendix Figure A4.8, Table A4.5), indicating that higher-elevation reaches consistently spawned later relative to their stream mean regardless of the fixed-effect temperature signal. Significant negative relationships between elevation and random slopes were detected in four streams (Appendix Figure A4.8, Table A4.5), indicating that higher-elevation reaches also tended to show stronger negative thermal responsiveness. The clearest patterns emerged in Big Creek and Camas Creek, which combine the widest elevation ranges among sampled streams with the largest reach sample sizes; both streams showed significant relationships in both models. Full regression statistics are reported in Appendix Table A4.5.
Discussion    
We analyzed a highly spatially and temporally resolved dataset of more 3,000 Chinook salmon redds across eight streams and four years to investigate Chinook salmon phenotypic diversity and the environmental determinants of spawn timing. A natural spline formulation of antecedent 90-day mean water temperature—capturing a strong nonlinear increase in spawn timing that leveled off at warm sites—emerged as the dominant population-level predictor. Our final linear mixed-effects models explained 60% of variance attributable to fixed effects and 99% when reach-level random effects were included (marginal R² = 0.599; conditional R² = 0.990), with RMSE of 1.71 days — performance that compares favorably to similar studies (e.g., Lisi et al. 2013). The high ICC (0.98) underscores that reach identity is the single strongest organizing principle of spawn timing variation: individual reaches have characteristic timing signatures that persist across years, shaped by fixed and dynamic habitat attributes, local thermal regimes, and unmeasured hydrologic and geomorphic processes. Reach-specific random slopes revealed pronounced heterogeneity in thermal sensitivity, with a strong negative intercept-slope correlation (ρ₀₁ = −0.83) indicating that earlier-spawning, higher-elevation reaches are substantially more thermally responsive than later-spawning ones. Together, these results demonstrate that fine-scale habitat heterogeneity, particularly thermal heterogeneity mediated by local geomorphic and hydrologic processes—structures diverse Chinook salmon spatial and temporal spawning portfolios at both stream and reach scales. 
Multiple studies have proposed a causal relation between the thermal regime during incubation and the time of adult spawning (Heggberget 1988; Brannon et al. 2005). As Brannon stated, “The controlling mechanism in spawn timing, however, is the timing of fry emergence, which translates into fitness.” Our analysis of different pre- and post- spawn metrics, however, does not support the proposed incubation-spawn timing relationship. We suggest that habitat availability and survival during spawning may also be important selective forces in the pre-spawn and spawn life stages. We also found temperature at individual spawning sites to be highly correlated throughout the year, suggesting a latent effect whereby site-level thermal regime is a persistent signature rather than a proximate cue operating through a specific seasonal window. Our understanding of the mechanisms controlling salmon spawn timing would benefit from further evaluation of the incubation life stage; one promising approach would be to model developmental phenology using Middle Fork temperature data—or other locations with strong spawning records—with newly developed methods for predicting phenology in wild populations (Sparks et al. 2019; 2025).
The nonlinear thermal responses and reach-specific random slopes highlight diverse thermal sensitivities of salmon spawning across the landscape. The strong negative intercept-slope correlation indicates that fish spawning earlier in higher-elevation reaches tend to show stronger temperature dependence. Biologically, this pattern suggests that early-spawning populations are present more dynamic responses to temperature — they adjust spawn timing more closely to thermal cues and exhibit broader variation in response to temperature change. In contrast, lower-elevation and later-spawning populations may be constrained by other factors such as photoperiod, migration fatigue, or compressed spawning windows, resulting in diminished thermal responsiveness. Lower-elevation reaches may also be operating near thermal ceilings that limit further phenological advancement regardless of temperature signal. These contrasting patterns across the elevation gradient emphasize that even within a single watershed, thermal sensitivity is not uniform but varies with habitat context in ecologically meaningful ways.
The topographic variables we tested — discharge, elevation, and slope — were weak predictors of spawn timing and introduced effects that were rendered unstable by collinearity with temperature and confounding with stream identity. Elevation warrants particular attention. In raw data, higher-elevation reaches spawn earlier — a relationship largely mediated by the strong negative correlation between elevation and temperature. However, in multivariate models that include temperature, the residual effect of elevation on spawn timing is positive: once thermal variation is accounted for, higher-elevation reaches tend to spawn later, suggesting that elevation captures an additional, as-yet unidentified pathway influencing spawn timing that is independent of the thermal signal already in the model. This residual signal is corroborated by the within-stream random effects structure: after accounting for fixed-effect temperature, higher-elevation reaches within Big Creek and Camas Creek showed systematically later baseline spawn timing and stronger thermal responsiveness relative to their stream means (Appendix Figure A4.8). Critically, these within-stream elevation gradients are most interpretable in streams like Big Creek and Camas that span wide, continuous habitat gradients across several hundred meters of elevation — streams where salmon occupy a broad and connected range of reaches at different elevations. In contrast, streams with constrained or fragmented spawning habitat compressed into a narrow elevation band offer little leverage to detect or interpret elevation-dependent patterns. This distinction helps explain why elevation performs poorly as a basin-wide fixed effect — the habitat contexts across which it operates are too heterogeneous — while still emerging as a coherent organizing variable within individual drainages that span sufficient elevation range. Spatial Stream Network models (Isaak et al. 2013) may offer a more rigorous framework for disentangling topographic and geomorphic influences on reach-level processes across broader geographic scales, where elevation gradients are more consistently expressed across systems.

Abundance, phenotypic diversity, and the limits of our sample
A central question motivating this work is whether the spawning portfolio we documented represents the full phenotypic diversity this population can express, or whether it reflects a contracted diversity associated with historically low abundances. We hypothesize the latter. Between 1995 and 2022, adult salmon returns averaged 1–3% of mid-1960s abundances (Thurow et al. 2020), and from 2023–2025 that figure has not improved; index redd counts from 2023–2025 suggest cumulative returns remained at approximately 1–2% of mid-1960s abundances (M. Donovan, IDFG, personal communication). The population we sampled was far below the carrying capacity of available spawning habitat, leaving many suitable areas unoccupied.
Although few studies have directly examined the relationship between adult salmon returns and phenotypic diversity, there is evidence that broader spawning diversity is expressed when more adults return. Davis (2015) examined the duration of salmon availability as a food source across three orders of magnitude in spawner abundances and found that spawning salmon were available to predators up to 2.5 times longer at the largest compared to the smallest abundances—a result consistent with greater phenotypic diversity, specifically broader variation in spawn duration, at higher escapements. Our own data from the Middle Fork offer a suggestive parallel: in 2003, when adult returns were the largest recorded since the 1980s, five of 33 mainstem redds were constructed nearly 10 days later than the latest redds in prior years (24–26 September versus 15–17 September), and spawners were observed using larger cobble substrate in reaches not used in other years (Thurow et al. 2020). Buffington et al. (In review) confirmed this pattern spatially, predicting that as escapements increased, salmon expanded the range of substrate used and occupied more stream reaches within the Middle Fork. 
	Historical records are consistent with the pattern as well, though they must be interpreted cautiously. In the late nineteenth century, when an estimated 8–10 million Chinook salmon annually entered the Columbia River (Lichatowich and Mobrand 1995), spawn timing in the Middle Fork system appears to have extended considerably later into the fall than is observed today. Captain Bernard’s forces traveled down Big Creek in July 1879 and observed what appears to have been active spawning near Cabin Creek—weeks earlier than the contemporary August onset for summer-run fish in that reach (Minshall 2014). In adjacent South Fork Salmon River drainages, diary entries and oral histories from the 1890s–1930s describe salmon spawning in November, a phenology with no known contemporary counterpart (Burns 1992). We cannot rule out other explanations for these observations, but taken together with Davis (2015) and our own 2003 data, they are consistent with the hypothesis that the Middle Fork's current spawning portfolio, though already remarkably diverse, represents a contracted expression of the phenotypic range possible at higher abundances. Whether currently at-risk populations retain the genetic capacity to re-express absent life history types as abundances recover is an important empirical question — one that dam removal experiments like the Elwha (Pess et al. 2024; Peters et al. 2025) are beginning to address elsewhere.

Management and conservation implications
The Middle Fork represents a uniquely intact reference system for studying phenological processes that operate with minimal anthropogenic alteration. Its Chinook salmon and steelhead (Oncorhynchus mykiss) populations are wild and indigenous, unaltered by hatchery supplementation (Thurow et al. 2000). Natural disturbances—wildfire, floods, debris flows—continuously generate and maintain habitat diversity across the 7,330 km2 basin (Isaak et al. 2003, Thurow 2015). The 650 km of accessible tributaries and 170 km of accessible mainstem (Mallet 1974; Thurow 1985) provide a physical template for the expression of diverse phenotypes that is rare in the Columbia River basin; stream-type Chinook populations of this character persist in only 4% of the potential historical range (Thurow et al. 2000).
Our results reinforce the conservation argument for protecting the habitat heterogeneity that produces phenotypic diversity. Diverse temperature regimes within and among streams give rise to among-population life history diversity (Cordoleani et al. 2021), and the portfolio effect — wherein asynchronous spawning across spatially variable habitats buffers populations against environmental stochasticity — is considered essential for maintaining salmon stock abundance and resilience (Hilborn et al. 2003; Schindler et al. 2010; Hugentobler et al. 2024). The consumer dimension of this portfolio is also ecologically significant: variation in spawn timing across thermally heterogeneous reaches enables mobile consumers — bears, eagles, otters — to locate profitable foraging opportunities sequentially across the watershed rather than competing at a single synchronous pulse (Schindler et al. 2013). When spawning is spatially asynchronous at fine scales, consumers do not need to travel far to exploit temporal variation in resource availability (Schindler et al. 2013).
	Climate change adds urgency to these conservation priorities. Increasing water temperatures are predicted to reduce low elevation stream habitat across the interior Columbia basin, though some losses may be offset by warming of currently cold, high-elevation reaches (Isaak et al. 2016). Jacobs et al. (2021, 2022) assessed future climate scenarios for the Middle Fork and found that while many tributaries will remain cold water refuges, summer flow reductions are likely to erode this buffer and produce net habitat losses in some high-elevation drainages. The persistence of substantial high-quality habitat across most Middle Fork drainages, combined with the thermal heterogeneity we documented, suggests that a diverse portfolio approach remains viable here—but only if habitat heterogeneity and population abundance are maintained. As Sturrock et al. (2018) observed, in environments facing increasingly volatile futures it is prudent to promote and maintain diverse life-history portfolios, because diversity is the mechanism by which salmon populations spread mortality risk and resist extirpation (Den Boer 1968). 

Limitations 
The scale of our analysis, small sample sizes in a few streams, and a narrow range of adult salmon returns may have limited our ability to describe a more complete portfolio of Middle Fork salmon spawning phenologies. We collected data in six major drainages and two large tributaries; four additional major drainages and 160 km of the mainstem spawning habitat (Thurow et al. 2020) were not sampled, largely because of tributary remoteness and relatively few redds. Within the drainages sampled, we prioritized areas known to support a majority of redds, so except for Marsh Creek, our redd surveys encompassed a relatively small proportion of available habitats. Some surveys in Beaver, Sulphur, and Marsh creeks were limited to one or two counts, constraining our analysis in those streams and years. Finally, adult salmon returns from 2002-2005 ranged from 1.9% to 9.4% of mid-1960s abundances—a narrow range at the low end of historic variation. The population sampled was far below the carrying capacity of available spawning habitat, leaving many suitable areas unoccupied. Re-doing our analysis with substantially larger spawning populations might reveal expression of even broader phenotypic diversity than we detected. These limiting factors likely suppressed our ability to detect the full range of spawning portfolios expressed by salmon across the heterogenous Middle Fork landscape, resulting in a snapshot of very diverse, though likely incomplete, salmon spawning portfolios.
The analytical approach we employed carries an additional limitation worth noting. The high conditional R² and ICC of the final model reflect the degree to which reach-level random effects capture system-specific variation in spawn timing — a strength for describing the Middle Fork in depth, but one that limits extrapolation to other watersheds without refitting reach-level parameters. A simpler fixed-effects model incorporating elevation alongside temperature would sacrifice within-system precision but offer greater transferability across systems, and developing such a framework across broader geographic scales represents a worthwhile direction for future comparative work.

